{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789405805,"nonce":"QWs6Fp/yeXWptdnzSHvc2dNHfPo=","title":"Python: list.sort and sorted were insertion sorts","message":"`zb_list_sort_*`, `sort_by_*` and `sort_desc_*` in the built-in library\nwere insertion sorts: quadratic, so `pairs.sort(key=lambda p: p[0])`\nover 50,000 tuples took 250 s (5,000 took 0.2 s). CPython's sort is\nO(n log n).\n\nFix: a stable bottom-up merge sort (`lists::merge_sort` in the DSL)\nshared by the three, with a scratch copy per pass and the keys moving\nalongside the elements for the keyed form; the direction is chosen once\nat the top of `sort_by` rather than inside the comparison. 40,000\nkeyed tuples sort in 0.19 s, 200,000 ints in 0.03 s.","files":null}]}